.TH "DELETE-OBJECTS"  "" "" ""
.SH NAME
delete-objects \- 
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
This operation enables you to delete multiple objects from a bucket using a single HTTP request. If you know the object keys that you want to delete, then this operation provides a suitable alternative to sending individual delete requests, reducing per\-request overhead.
.sp
The request can contain a list of up to 1,000 keys that you want to delete. In the XML, you provide the object key names, and optionally, version IDs if you want to delete a specific version of the object from a versioning\-enabled bucket. For each key, Amazon S3 performs a delete operation and returns the result of that delete, success or failure, in the response. If the object specified in the request isn\(aqt found, Amazon S3 confirms the deletion by returning the result as deleted.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDirectory buckets\fP \- S3 Versioning isn\(aqt enabled and supported for directory buckets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDirectory buckets\fP \- For directory buckets, you must make requests for this API operation to the Zonal endpoint. These endpoints support virtual\-hosted\-style requests in the format 
.nf
\(ga\(ga
.fi
\fI\%https://\fP\fIamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\fP .s3express\-\fIzone\-id\fP .*region\-code* .amazonaws.com/\fIkey\-name\fP \(ga\(ga . Path\-style requests are not supported. For more information about endpoints in Availability Zones, see \fI\%Regional and Zonal endpoints for directory buckets in Availability Zones\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP . For more information about endpoints in Local Zones, see \fI\%Concepts for directory buckets in Local Zones\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.IP "System Message: WARNING/2 (<string>:, line 33)"
Inline literal start\-string without end\-string.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The operation supports two modes for the response: verbose and quiet. By default, the operation uses verbose mode in which the response includes the result of deletion of each key in your request. In quiet mode the response includes only keys where the delete operation encountered an error. For a successful deletion in a quiet mode, the operation does not return any information about the delete in the response body.
.sp
When performing this action on an MFA Delete enabled bucket, that attempts to delete any versioned objects, you must include an MFA token. If you do not provide one, the entire request will fail, even if there are non\-versioned objects you are trying to delete. If you provide an invalid token, whether there are versioned keys in the request or not, the entire Multi\-Object Delete request will fail. For information about MFA Delete, see \fI\%MFA Delete\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBDirectory buckets\fP \- MFA delete is not supported by directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Permissions
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGeneral purpose bucket permissions\fP \- The following permissions are required in your policies when your \fBDeleteObjects\fP request includes specific headers.
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2

.nf
**
.fi
\fBs3:DeleteObject\fP ** \- To delete an object from a bucket, you must always specify the \fBs3:DeleteObject\fP permission.
.IP "System Message: WARNING/2 (<string>:, line 62)"
Inline strong start\-string without end\-string.
.IP \(bu 2

.nf
**
.fi
\fBs3:DeleteObjectVersion\fP ** \- To delete a specific version of an object from a versioning\-enabled bucket, you must specify the \fBs3:DeleteObjectVersion\fP permission.
.IP "System Message: WARNING/2 (<string>:, line 64)"
Inline strong start\-string without end\-string.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDirectory bucket permissions\fP \- To grant access to this API operation on a directory bucket, we recommend that you use the \(ga \fBCreateSession\fP \fI\%https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/API_CreateSession\fP\&.html\(ga__ API operation for session\-based authorization. Specifically, you grant the \fBs3express:CreateSession\fP permission to the directory bucket in a bucket policy or an IAM identity\-based policy. Then, you make the \fBCreateSession\fP API call on the bucket to obtain a session token. With the session token in your request header, you can make API requests to this operation. After the session token expires, you make another \fBCreateSession\fP API call to generate a new session token for use. Amazon Web Services CLI or SDKs create session and refresh the session token automatically to avoid service interruptions when a session expires. For more information about authorization, see \(ga \fBCreateSession\fP \fI\%https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/API/API_CreateSession\fP\&.html\(ga__ .
.sp
Content\-MD5 request header
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGeneral purpose bucket\fP \- The Content\-MD5 request header is required for all Multi\-Object Delete requests. Amazon S3 uses the header value to ensure that your request body has not been altered in transit.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDirectory bucket\fP \- The Content\-MD5 request header or a additional checksum request header (including \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-crc32\fP , \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-crc32c\fP , \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-sha1\fP , or \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-sha256\fP ) is required for all Multi\-Object Delete requests.
.sp
HTTP Host header syntax
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBDirectory buckets\fP \- The HTTP Host header syntax is \(ga\(ga \fIBucket\-name\fP .s3express\-\fIzone\-id\fP .*region\-code* .amazonaws.com\(ga\(ga .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following operations are related to \fBDeleteObjects\fP :
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%CreateMultipartUpload\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%UploadPart\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%CompleteMultipartUpload\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%ListParts\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%AbortMultipartUpload\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
See also: AWS API Documentation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
  delete\-objects
\-\-bucket <value>
\-\-delete <value>
[\-\-mfa <value>]
[\-\-request\-payer <value>]
[\-\-bypass\-governance\-retention | \-\-no\-bypass\-governance\-retention]
[\-\-expected\-bucket\-owner <value>]
[\-\-checksum\-algorithm <value>]
[\-\-cli\-input\-json | \-\-cli\-input\-yaml]
[\-\-generate\-cli\-skeleton <value>]
[\-\-debug]
[\-\-endpoint\-url <value>]
[\-\-no\-verify\-ssl]
[\-\-no\-paginate]
[\-\-output <value>]
[\-\-query <value>]
[\-\-profile <value>]
[\-\-region <value>]
[\-\-version <value>]
[\-\-color <value>]
[\-\-no\-sign\-request]
[\-\-ca\-bundle <value>]
[\-\-cli\-read\-timeout <value>]
[\-\-cli\-connect\-timeout <value>]
[\-\-cli\-binary\-format <value>]
[\-\-no\-cli\-pager]
[\-\-cli\-auto\-prompt]
[\-\-no\-cli\-auto\-prompt]
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH OPTIONS
.sp
\fB\-\-bucket\fP (string) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The bucket name containing the objects to delete.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBDirectory buckets\fP \- When you use this operation with a directory bucket, you must use virtual\-hosted\-style requests in the format \(ga\(ga \fIBucket\-name\fP .s3express\-\fIzone\-id\fP .*region\-code* .amazonaws.com\(ga\(ga . Path\-style requests are not supported. Directory bucket names must be unique in the chosen Zone (Availability Zone or Local Zone). Bucket names must follow the format \(ga\(ga \fIbucket\-base\-name\fP \-\-\fIzone\-id\fP \-\-x\-s3\(ga\(ga (for example, \(ga\(ga \fIamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\fP \-\-\fIusw2\-az1\fP \-\-x\-s3\(ga\(ga ). For information about bucket naming restrictions, see \fI\%Directory bucket naming rules\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
\fBAccess points\fP \- When you use this action with an access point for general purpose buckets, you must provide the alias of the access point in place of the bucket name or specify the access point ARN. When you use this action with an access point for directory buckets, you must provide the access point name in place of the bucket name. When using the access point ARN, you must direct requests to the access point hostname. The access point hostname takes the form \fIAccessPointName\fP \-\fIAccountId\fP .s3\-accesspoint.*Region* .amazonaws.com. When using this action with an access point through the Amazon Web Services SDKs, you provide the access point ARN in place of the bucket name. For more information about access point ARNs, see \fI\%Using access points\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Object Lambda access points are not supported by directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBS3 on Outposts\fP \- When you use this action with S3 on Outposts, you must direct requests to the S3 on Outposts hostname. The S3 on Outposts hostname takes the form \(ga\(ga \fIAccessPointName\fP \-\fIAccountId\fP .*outpostID* .s3\-outposts.*Region* .amazonaws.com\(ga\(ga . When you use this action with S3 on Outposts, the destination bucket must be the Outposts access point ARN or the access point alias. For more information about S3 on Outposts, see \fI\%What is S3 on Outposts?\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-delete\fP (structure) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Container for the request.
.sp
Objects \-> (list) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The object to delete.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBDirectory buckets\fP \- For directory buckets, an object that\(aqs composed entirely of whitespace characters is not supported by the \fBDeleteObjects\fP API operation. The request will receive a \fB400 Bad Request\fP error and none of the objects in the request will be deleted.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Object Identifier is unique value to identify objects.
.sp
Key \-> (string) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Key name of the object.
.sp
\fBWARNING:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Replacement must be made for object keys containing special characters (such as carriage returns) when using XML requests. For more information, see \fI\%XML related object key constraints\fP .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Constraints:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
min: \fB1\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
VersionId \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Version ID for the specific version of the object to delete.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
ETag \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
An entity tag (ETag) is an identifier assigned by a web server to a specific version of a resource found at a URL. This header field makes the request method conditional on \fBETags\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Entity tags (ETags) for S3 Express One Zone are random alphanumeric strings unique to the object.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
LastModifiedTime \-> (timestamp)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If present, the objects are deleted only if its modification times matches the provided \fBTimestamp\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is only supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Size \-> (long)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If present, the objects are deleted only if its size matches the provided size in bytes.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is only supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Quiet \-> (boolean)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Element to enable quiet mode for the request. When you add this element, you must set its value to \fBtrue\fP .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Shorthand Syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
Objects=[{Key=string,VersionId=string,ETag=string,LastModifiedTime=timestamp,Size=long},{Key=string,VersionId=string,ETag=string,LastModifiedTime=timestamp,Size=long}],Quiet=boolean
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
JSON Syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
  \(dqObjects\(dq: [
    {
      \(dqKey\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
      \(dqVersionId\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
      \(dqETag\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
      \(dqLastModifiedTime\(dq: timestamp,
      \(dqSize\(dq: long
    }
    ...
  ],
  \(dqQuiet\(dq: true|false
}
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-mfa\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The concatenation of the authentication device\(aqs serial number, a space, and the value that is displayed on your authentication device. Required to permanently delete a versioned object if versioning is configured with MFA delete enabled.
.sp
When performing the \fBDeleteObjects\fP operation on an MFA delete enabled bucket, which attempts to delete the specified versioned objects, you must include an MFA token. If you don\(aqt provide an MFA token, the entire request will fail, even if there are non\-versioned objects that you are trying to delete. If you provide an invalid token, whether there are versioned object keys in the request or not, the entire Multi\-Object Delete request will fail. For information about MFA Delete, see \fI\%MFA Delete\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-request\-payer\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Confirms that the requester knows that they will be charged for the request. Bucket owners need not specify this parameter in their requests. If either the source or destination S3 bucket has Requester Pays enabled, the requester will pay for corresponding charges to copy the object. For information about downloading objects from Requester Pays buckets, see \fI\%Downloading Objects in Requester Pays Buckets\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrequester\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-bypass\-governance\-retention\fP | \fB\-\-no\-bypass\-governance\-retention\fP (boolean)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Specifies whether you want to delete this object even if it has a Governance\-type Object Lock in place. To use this header, you must have the \fBs3:BypassGovernanceRetention\fP permission.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-expected\-bucket\-owner\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The account ID of the expected bucket owner. If the account ID that you provide does not match the actual owner of the bucket, the request fails with the HTTP status code \fB403 Forbidden\fP (access denied).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-checksum\-algorithm\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Indicates the algorithm used to create the checksum for the object when you use the SDK. This header will not provide any additional functionality if you don\(aqt use the SDK. When you send this header, there must be a corresponding \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-*algorithm* \(ga\(ga or \(ga\(gax\-amz\-trailer\fP header sent. Otherwise, Amazon S3 fails the request with the HTTP status code \fB400 Bad Request\fP .
.sp
For the 
.nf
\(ga\(ga
.fi
x\-amz\-checksum\-\fIalgorithm\fP \(ga\(ga header, replace \(ga\(ga \fIalgorithm\fP \(ga\(ga with the supported algorithm from the following list:
.IP "System Message: WARNING/2 (<string>:, line 439)"
Inline literal start\-string without end\-string.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC32\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC32C\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC64NVME\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA1\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA256\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information, see \fI\%Checking object integrity\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
If the individual checksum value you provide through \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-*algorithm* \(ga\(ga doesn\(aqt match the checksum algorithm you set through \(ga\(gax\-amz\-sdk\-checksum\-algorithm\fP , Amazon S3 fails the request with a \fBBadDigest\fP error.
.sp
If you provide an individual checksum, Amazon S3 ignores any provided \fBChecksumAlgorithm\fP parameter.
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC32\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC32C\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA1\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA256\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC64NVME\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-input\-json\fP | \fB\-\-cli\-input\-yaml\fP (string)
Reads arguments from the JSON string provided. The JSON string follows the format provided by \fB\-\-generate\-cli\-skeleton\fP\&. If other arguments are provided on the command line, those values will override the JSON\-provided values. It is not possible to pass arbitrary binary values using a JSON\-provided value as the string will be taken literally. This may not be specified along with \fB\-\-cli\-input\-yaml\fP\&.
.sp
\fB\-\-generate\-cli\-skeleton\fP (string)
Prints a JSON skeleton to standard output without sending an API request. If provided with no value or the value \fBinput\fP, prints a sample input JSON that can be used as an argument for \fB\-\-cli\-input\-json\fP\&. Similarly, if provided \fByaml\-input\fP it will print a sample input YAML that can be used with \fB\-\-cli\-input\-yaml\fP\&. If provided with the value \fBoutput\fP, it validates the command inputs and returns a sample output JSON for that command. The generated JSON skeleton is not stable between versions of the AWS CLI and there are no backwards compatibility guarantees in the JSON skeleton generated.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.sp
\fB\-\-debug\fP (boolean)
.sp
Turn on debug logging.
.sp
\fB\-\-endpoint\-url\fP (string)
.sp
Override command\(aqs default URL with the given URL.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-verify\-ssl\fP (boolean)
.sp
By default, the AWS CLI uses SSL when communicating with AWS services. For each SSL connection, the AWS CLI will verify SSL certificates. This option overrides the default behavior of verifying SSL certificates.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-paginate\fP (boolean)
.sp
Disable automatic pagination. If automatic pagination is disabled, the AWS CLI will only make one call, for the first page of results.
.sp
\fB\-\-output\fP (string)
.sp
The formatting style for command output.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
json
.IP \(bu 2
text
.IP \(bu 2
table
.IP \(bu 2
yaml
.IP \(bu 2
yaml\-stream
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-query\fP (string)
.sp
A JMESPath query to use in filtering the response data.
.sp
\fB\-\-profile\fP (string)
.sp
Use a specific profile from your credential file.
.sp
\fB\-\-region\fP (string)
.sp
The region to use. Overrides config/env settings.
.sp
\fB\-\-version\fP (string)
.sp
Display the version of this tool.
.sp
\fB\-\-color\fP (string)
.sp
Turn on/off color output.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
on
.IP \(bu 2
off
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-sign\-request\fP (boolean)
.sp
Do not sign requests. Credentials will not be loaded if this argument is provided.
.sp
\fB\-\-ca\-bundle\fP (string)
.sp
The CA certificate bundle to use when verifying SSL certificates. Overrides config/env settings.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-read\-timeout\fP (int)
.sp
The maximum socket read time in seconds. If the value is set to 0, the socket read will be blocking and not timeout. The default value is 60 seconds.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-connect\-timeout\fP (int)
.sp
The maximum socket connect time in seconds. If the value is set to 0, the socket connect will be blocking and not timeout. The default value is 60 seconds.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-binary\-format\fP (string)
.sp
The formatting style to be used for binary blobs. The default format is base64. The base64 format expects binary blobs to be provided as a base64 encoded string. The raw\-in\-base64\-out format preserves compatibility with AWS CLI V1 behavior and binary values must be passed literally. When providing contents from a file that map to a binary blob \fBfileb://\fP will always be treated as binary and use the file contents directly regardless of the \fBcli\-binary\-format\fP setting. When using \fBfile://\fP the file contents will need to properly formatted for the configured \fBcli\-binary\-format\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
base64
.IP \(bu 2
raw\-in\-base64\-out
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-cli\-pager\fP (boolean)
.sp
Disable cli pager for output.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-auto\-prompt\fP (boolean)
.sp
Automatically prompt for CLI input parameters.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-cli\-auto\-prompt\fP (boolean)
.sp
Disable automatically prompt for CLI input parameters.
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To use the following examples, you must have the AWS CLI installed and configured. See the \fI\%Getting started guide\fP in the \fIAWS CLI User Guide\fP for more information.
.sp
Unless otherwise stated, all examples have unix\-like quotation rules. These examples will need to be adapted to your terminal\(aqs quoting rules. See \fI\%Using quotation marks with strings\fP in the \fIAWS CLI User Guide\fP .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following command deletes an object from a bucket named \fBamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
aws s3api delete\-objects \-\-bucket amzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket \-\-delete file://delete.json
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBdelete.json\fP is a JSON document in the current directory that specifies the object to delete:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
  \(dqObjects\(dq: [
    {
      \(dqKey\(dq: \(dqtest1.txt\(dq
    }
  ],
  \(dqQuiet\(dq: false
}
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Output:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
    \(dqDeleted\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqDeleteMarkerVersionId\(dq: \(dqmYAT5Mc6F7aeUL8SS7FAAqUPO1koHwzU\(dq,
            \(dqKey\(dq: \(dqtest1.txt\(dq,
            \(dqDeleteMarker\(dq: true
        }
    ]
}
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH OUTPUT
.sp
Deleted \-> (list)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Container element for a successful delete. It identifies the object that was successfully deleted.
.sp
(structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Information about the deleted object.
.sp
Key \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The name of the deleted object.
.sp
Constraints:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
min: \fB1\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
VersionId \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The version ID of the deleted object.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
DeleteMarker \-> (boolean)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Indicates whether the specified object version that was permanently deleted was (true) or was not (false) a delete marker before deletion. In a simple DELETE, this header indicates whether (true) or not (false) the current version of the object is a delete marker. To learn more about delete markers, see \fI\%Working with delete markers\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
DeleteMarkerVersionId \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The version ID of the delete marker created as a result of the DELETE operation. If you delete a specific object version, the value returned by this header is the version ID of the object version deleted.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
RequestCharged \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If present, indicates that the requester was successfully charged for the request. For more information, see \fI\%Using Requester Pays buckets for storage transfers and usage\fP in the \fIAmazon Simple Storage Service user guide\fP .
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrequester\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
